export default "This guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## What the Luteal Phase Is and Why It Gets Its Own Attention\n\nMost people, when they think about their cycle, think about their period. The luteal phase (the roughly two weeks between ovulation and menstruation) is less visible but carries significant hormonal weight. It is the phase responsible for PMS symptoms, for maintaining any potential pregnancy, and for the characteristic pre-period mood and physical changes most people are familiar with.\n\nUnderstanding your luteal phase length is one of the more useful pieces of information you can get from cycle tracking beyond basic period prediction.\n\n## What a Typical Luteal Phase Looks Like\n\n### Duration\n\nThe luteal phase typically lasts 10 to 16 days. Unlike the follicular phase, which can stretch or compress depending on when ovulation occurs, the luteal phase is constrained by how long the corpus luteum survives before it degrades. That degradation timeline tends to be more consistent for a given person.\n\nMost people have a luteal phase of 11 to 14 days. If yours runs 10 days, that is within normal range. If it consistently runs under 10 days, that is worth knowing.\n\n### Hormonal arc\n\nIn the first half of the luteal phase, progesterone rises steadily as the corpus luteum is active. Estrogen has a secondary rise as well. In the second half, typically the week before your period, progesterone and estrogen both begin to fall as the corpus luteum breaks down. That fall is what triggers PMS symptoms and eventually menstruation.\n\n## How to Calculate Your Luteal Phase Length\n\n**Step 1:** Identify when you ovulated. The most reliable methods are:\n- LH test strips (ovulation predictor kits): the surge in LH typically occurs 24 to 36 hours before ovulation\n- Basal body temperature charting: BBT rises slightly after ovulation due to progesterone; this confirms ovulation has occurred but does not predict it in advance\n- Fertility monitors that track multiple hormone markers\n\n**Step 2:** Note the first day of your next period.\n\n**Step 3:** Count the days from ovulation to the day before your period started (some sources count through the day your period started; the approach matters less than being consistent).\n\nDo this across several cycles. Your average luteal phase length is more informative than any single cycle.\n\n## What a Short Luteal Phase May Indicate\n\nA luteal phase shorter than 10 days is sometimes described clinically as a short luteal phase. The significance depends on context:\n\n- For people tracking cycles generally: a short luteal phase is useful information about your cycle pattern but may have no clinical implications in isolation.\n- For people trying to conceive: the luteal phase length matters because implantation requires the uterine lining to be maintained by progesterone for long enough to allow a fertilized egg to implant. Very short luteal phases are associated with difficulty maintaining very early pregnancies in some cases. This is an area to discuss with a reproductive endocrinologist or OB-GYN rather than draw conclusions from self-tracking alone.\n- For people with heavy premenstrual symptoms: cycle length and phase structure are useful clinical data when evaluating hormonal health.\n\nA short luteal phase identified through tracking is a data point, not a diagnosis.\n\n## What a Long Luteal Phase May Indicate\n\nA luteal phase longer than 16 days is less common. If the luteal phase is unusually prolonged, potential causes include:\n\n- Pregnancy: the corpus luteum continues producing progesterone past its normal lifespan when a pregnancy is established, which delays menstruation.\n- Hormonal variation: some cycles produce a more persistent corpus luteum without pregnancy.\n\nIf you expect your period and it is late, and you have had unprotected sex, a pregnancy test is the appropriate first step.\n\n## Tracking the Luteal Phase Over Time\n\nIf you track both ovulation timing and period start dates across multiple cycles, you can calculate your luteal phase for each cycle and see whether it is consistent. Most people find their luteal phase length is relatively stable, varying by a day or two.\n\nSignificant changes, such as a luteal phase that used to be 13 days and is now consistently 8, are the kind of shift worth noting and raising with a provider.\n\n### What to log during the luteal phase\n\nBeyond dates, useful luteal phase data includes:\n- Daily mood on a consistent scale (this is the window where PMDD and PMS symptoms occur)\n- Physical symptoms: bloating, breast tenderness, fatigue, headaches\n- Sleep quality\n- Any cramping or pain separate from menstruation\n- Energy levels\n\nA detailed luteal phase log over several cycles gives you a pattern, not just a data point.\n\n## The Privacy Context\n\nLuteal phase data, combined with ovulation tracking, reveals detailed information about your reproductive status, fertility patterns, and cycle health. If this data is stored on an app's servers, it is subject to the same exposures as all cloud-stored health data: breaches, subpoenas, and platform data-sharing arrangements.\n\nFlo Health's 2021 FTC settlement showed that period and cycle data was shared with third-party analytics platforms despite user expectations. Period tracker data is not covered by HIPAA. Apps that keep data only on your device remove that exposure entirely, which matters especially when the data is as detailed as luteal phase and ovulation tracking.";
